      "text": "To find the distance from the origin (0, 0, 0) to the point with known values of \\( x \\) and \\( z \\):\n\n$$\n\n4 - 0 = 2, 1 - 0 = 1, -2 - 0 = -2\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nThen, by Pythagoras theorem, distance \\( d \\) from the origin to the point\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\n&d^2 = 2^2 + 1^2 + (-2)^2\\\\\n\n\\\\\\\\\n\nd^2 &= 4 + 1 + 4 \\\\\n\n\\normalsize d &= \\sqrt{9} \\\\\n\n\\end{align*}\n\n$$\n\nThis distance is also the \\( y \\)-coordinate of another point on the same line. Now, finding the \\( z-\\)coordinate of this new point.\n\nStep 1: To find the \\( y \\)-coordinate of the point, find the slope between the given points and translate the final point to the \\( y \\) value:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\n&y - 1 = \\dfrac{(1-1)}{(5-2)} \\longrightarrow y - 1 = 0 \\\\\n\n\\normalsize y = 1\n\n\\end{align*}\n\nAlso,\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\n&z - 1 = \\dfrac{(1-1)}{(5-2)} \\longrightarrow z - 1 = 0 \\\\\n\n\\normalsize z = 1\n\n\\end{align*}\n\nStep 2: Next, you already know the \\( x \\)-coordinate and the \\( y \\) and \\( z \\)-coordinates of the point to calculate the \\( z \\) coordinate.\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\n&4 - 0 = 2, 1 - 0 = 1, -2 - 0 = -2\n\n\\end{align*}\n\nBy Pythagorus\n\nTo conclude...\n\n$$\n\n\\boxed{1}\n\n$$\n\nThank you, Can you please show me how to solve problems involving the Pythagorean theorem, assuming only one value is to be determined.\n\nThe Pythagorean theorem states that in a right-angled triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse is equal to the sum of the squares of the other two sides. The formula for the Pythagorean theorem is: $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$, where $a$ and $b$ are the lengths of the legs of the triangle and $c$ is the length of the hypotenuse. To solve a problem involving the Pythagorean theorem, follow these steps:\n\n1. Identify the right triangle in the problem and label the sides correctly, with the hypotenuse as the longest side.\n2. Substitute the known values for $a$, $b$, and $c$ into the Pythagorean theorem.\n3. Solve for the unknown variable by isolating it on one side of the equation and simplifying.\n\nExample:\n\nFind the length of the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs of lengths 3 and 4.\n\nSolution:\n1. A right triangle with legs of lengths 3 and 4 is labeled correctly.\n2. Substitute $a=3$, $b=4$, and solve for $c$ using the Pythagorean theorem:\n$$ 3^2 + 4^2 = c^2 \\longrightarrow 9 + 16 = c^2 \\longrightarrow c = \\sqrt{25} = 5$$\n\nThe length of the hypotenuse is $\\boxed{5}$.\n\nCould you provide an example of a practical application of the Pythagorean theorem in real life?\n\nSure! Here is an example of a practical application of the Pythagorean theorem in real life:\n\nSuppose that you are setting up a tent and you place two poles that are perpendicular to the ground. You want to hang a rope between the two poles that is the shortest distance possible between them. The length of the pole is 10 feet, while the second pole is 12 feet tall. You want to find the length of the rope needed to hang it between the two poles so that you minimize the amount of rope used.\n\nLet's break this problem down using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\n1. Draw a diagram of the situation. Label the 10-foot pole as \"P1\" and the 12-foot pole as \"P2\". The rope is \"P1P2,\" and we need to find the length of \"P1P2.\"\n2. The distance between the bases of the two poles (10 + 12 = 22 feet) forms one leg of a right triangle. The height of the pair of poles (12 feet) is the second leg of the right triangle.\n3. To find the distance between the tops of the poles (which is the hypotenuse of the right triangle), use the Pythagorean theorem: $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$, where $a$ and $b$ are the lengths of the legs of the triangle, and $c$ is the length of the hypotenuse. In this case, $a$ is 12 feet, and $b$ is 10 feet, so we have: \n\t$(\"P1P2\")^2 = (12 feet)^2 + (10 feet)^2 = 576+ 100 = 676$.\n4. The length of the rope is the square root of 676, (sqrt(676)) = 26 feet.\n5. Therefore, you can calculate that the length of the rope needed between the two poles is 26 feet.\n\nWow, that makes sense! Let me try it out with my own example. Suppose I have a right triangle with one leg measuring 7 feet and another leg measuring 11 feet. What is the length of the hypotenuse?\n\nSure! You can use the Pythagorean theorem to solve the problem. The formula for the Pythagorean theorem is:\n\n$$\n\na^2 + b^2 = c^2\n\n$$\n\nwhere a and b are the lengths of the legs of the triangle and c is the length of the hypotenuse.\n\nIn your example, one leg has a length of 7 feet and another leg has a length of 11 feet.\n\nSo, using the Pythagorean theorem, you can calculate the length of the hypotenuse as follows:\n\n$$\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\n8^2 + 11^2 &= c^2 \\\\\n\n64 + 121 &= c^2 \\\\\n\n185 &= c^2 \\\\\n\n\\sqrt{185} &= c \\\\\n\n\\end{align*}\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the hypotenuse is 13.6 conclusively. \n\nIn other words, if you have a right triangle where one of your legs measures 7 feet and another leg measures 11 feet, then the length of the hypotenuse would be approximately 13.6 feet.",
